## Branding Rules for Flashy Reports (v22)

- **Neo default (no BRAND_CONFIG)**: Grey gradient cover, white accent text. No hardcoded brand color.
- **Branded reports**: When BRAND_CONFIG is set, the agent MUST look up the brand's actual colors via web_search (e.g. "{company_name} brand color hex") and set BRAND_CONFIG["primary_color"] accordingly. That primary_color then drives:
  - `accent_color` parameter on GradientCoverPage/GradientClosePage → pass BRAND_CONFIG["primary_color"] (decorative elements only)
  - metric_columns() value text → ALWAYS use TEXT_ON_BRAND, NEVER raw BRAND_CONFIG["primary_color"]. Validate with assert_readable().
  - Cover/close gradient stops — derived from primary_color via the darken() helper
  - Table header backgrounds — via styled_table(header_bg=primary_color)
- **Cover**: Always use GradientCoverPage with the full-page gradient. Supply report_type, summary_text, and metrics for a complete hero page.
- **Close page**: Add GradientClosePage as the last page for a visual bookend effect.
- **Cover KPIs**: Use metric_columns() on cover pages — the vertical grid with big accent-colored numbers and thin dividers. Do NOT use metric_cards() on dark gradient backgrounds.
- **Interior KPIs**: metric_cards() is still correct for interior pages with the light #F8F8F8 background.
- **Table severity columns**: When using badge() cells, do NOT also apply highlight_col to the same column — badges already provide visual distinction and a dark column background behind them looks cluttered. Reserve highlight_col for plain numeric or text columns only.
- **Severity badges**: Use badge() for colored pill labels. Always use semantic severity colors (CRITICAL=#B30000, HIGH=#CC3300, MEDIUM=#E07020, LOW=#0066CC).
- **No hardcoded accent color**: The accent color is always derived from the brand at report generation time. Neo's default is white text on grey gradient.

## CONTENT RULES (mandatory for all text written into the PDF)

These rules govern content writing and layout decisions. They do NOT override any component code, colors, or styles defined above.

### Rule 1 — Accessibility (WCAG AA minimum)

- NEVER use text below 9pt for any element.
- NEVER use light gray text on white background.
- NEVER use color alone to convey meaning. Add shape, icon, or label alongside color.
- Every image MUST have alt text or caption.
- Minimum contrast: 4.5:1 normal text, 3:1 large text.

### Rule 2 — Readability

- Target 10 words per sentence. Max 15.
- One idea per sentence. No compound clauses.
- Use bullet lists for 3+ related items.
- Prefer active voice over passive voice.
- Use plain language. Avoid jargon unless defined.
- Numbers over words for quantities (use "5" not "five").
- Front-load key info in each sentence.
- Avoid orphan words on lines by themselves.
- Paragraphs: max 4 sentences. Break after that.
- Use title case for headings and titles (e.g., "Executive Summary").
- Use sentence case for body text, labels, and captions.
- NEVER use ALL CAPS for any text element.

### Rule 3 — Gestalt Layout Principles

- Inter-section spacing must be at least 2x intra-section spacing. (If paragraphs are 8pt apart, sections must be 16pt+ apart.)
- Related items cluster together. Unrelated items separate.
- Left-align ALL text: titles, headings, subtitles, and body. Never center titles or headings. Left alignment aids scanning.
- Consistent indentation: 18pt per hierarchy level.
- Never strand a heading at page bottom without at least 2 lines of body below it. Use keepWithNext on all headings.

## MANDATORY RULES

1. EVERY table cell MUST use Paragraph objects via make_row(). NEVER raw strings.
2. EVERY table MUST go through styled_table() which returns RoundedTable. NEVER raw Table in story.
3. Key metrics (2-6 counts/percentages) MUST use metric_cards(). NEVER a table for metrics.
4. 3+ comparable numeric values MUST include a chart (bar, pie, or horizontal bar).
5. Column widths MUST be proportional fractions of DOC_WIDTH that sum to DOC_WIDTH.
6. wordWrap MUST be None. splitLongWords MUST be 0. NEVER use wordWrap='CJK'.
7. ALL headings MUST have keepWithNext=True.
8. Body text 11pt. Cell text 9pt. Minimum text size 9pt. NEVER smaller.
9. After writing the Python script, ALWAYS run it and verify with: pdftoppm -r 150 -png output.pdf tmp/preview
10. Then read_file each preview PNG to visually inspect. Fix any issues before delivering.
11. Color palette is FIXED. Never change hex values. Never use pastel alternatives.
12. REPORT_TITLE MUST be set before doc.multiBuild(). Use the report's display name (e.g. "Subdomain Discovery Report").
13. GradientCoverPage does NOT accept footer_text. Footer is unified via onPageEnd on BOTH page templates.
14. ALL bar charts MUST include value labels at the end of each bar. Horizontal bars show values at the RIGHT end; vertical bars show values at the TOP. Use barLabelFormat + barLabels properties (nudge, fontName, fontSize, fillColor). NEVER create a bar chart without visible value labels.
15. MUST use doc.multiBuild(story) instead of doc.build(story). multiBuild performs a two-pass build so that TableOfContents page numbers resolve correctly. Using doc.build() will result in a TOC with no page numbers.
16. custom_logo_text is OPTIONAL. When generating a branded report with BRAND_CONFIG, set custom_logo_text=BRAND_CONFIG["company_name"] as the text fallback when no logo image is available. When not branded, ALWAYS default to None (keeps "neo by Projectdiscovery"). Never assume the user wants a custom logo unless they explicitly request a branded report.
17. TEXT OVERLAP PREVENTION (STRICT): Column widths must be generous enough for ALL content including badges with padding. Badge columns need minimum 80pt width. Long text columns MUST use Paragraph() so text wraps naturally within cell bounds. Test column layout: total of colWidths must equal DOC_WIDTH exactly. If a table has badges + long titles, allocate at least 55% of DOC_WIDTH to the title column and reduce other columns proportionally.
18. For KV-style tables (label + value), use styles["cell_value"] for the value column to ensure strong contrast against the light grey background. styles["cell"] (70% black) is for auxiliary data; styles["cell_value"] (full dark #1A1A1A) is for primary values the reader must see at a glance.
19. CONTRAST VALIDATION (MANDATORY): Every text element drawn on a gradient or colored background MUST pass WCAG AA contrast (4.5:1 normal, 3:1 large/36pt+). Use `assert_readable(fg_hex, bg_hex)` to validate at generation time. metric_columns() values on dark gradients are ALWAYS white (#FFFFFF); on light gradients ALWAYS dark (#1A1A1A). The accent_color parameter on cover/close pages is for decorative elements (rule lines, section labels) — NEVER use it as the text color for metric values. If in doubt, white text on dark gradient, dark text on light gradient.

